  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nOur weather pattern for Thursday, looks very similar to \nWednesday, so to no one's surprise we will have very similar \nweather. Most of the short range models bring another round of \nevening showers and thunderstorms across the area. With the best \nchances for rain being a little west of the Rio Grande. The main \nplayers in the weather on Wednesday, which was a ridge over the \nsoutheast U.S. and a trough out west, don't move much on Thursday,\nso that is why the weather will be very similar. By Friday the \ntrough to our west will begin to push east and that will shift the\nbetter rain chances east of the Rio Grande for Friday and \nSaturday evenings. Temperatures all three days will run at or just\na little above average.\n\nBy Sunday the trough to our west will have moved to our east and \nsweep in lots of drier air behind it. So Sunday into Monday, looks\nlike a pretty quiet couple of days with little chances for rain \nand high temperature near average. But the dry weather won't last,\nas another west coast trough approaches the region while at the \nsame time an upper level ridge will still be to our east. Much \nlike the last couple of days, the circulation around the ridge and\ntrough will begin to draw up abundant moisture into our area. If \nthe extended GFS is to be believed, we will have a threat for more\nheavy rain for Tuesday and Wednesday, while the extended ECMWF \nhas good chances for rain, but is not as bullish on the potential \nfor excessive heavy rain. High temperatures on next Tuesday and \nWednesday will depend greatly on how much clouds and rain we see, \nbut the GFS is hinting at some below average high temperatures. \nThat would be a nice change after our long hot summer.",
